      "text": "To solve the system of equations \\(x + y = 4\\) and \\(3xy = 4\\), we start by expressing \\(y\\) in terms of \\(x\\) from the first equation. From \\(x + y = 4\\), we get \\(y = 4 - x\\). Substituting this into the second equation, we have:\n\n\\[3x(4 - x) = 4.\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[12x - 3x^2 = 4.\\]\n\nRearranging the terms gives us a quadratic equation:\n\n\\[3x^2 - 12x + 4 = 0.\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 3\\), \\(b = -12\\), and \\(c = 4\\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\n\\[x = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4}}{2 \\cdot 3} = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 48}}{6} = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{96}}{6} = \\frac{12 \\pm 4\\sqrt{6}}{6} = 2 \\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\]\n\nThus, the solutions for \\(x\\) are:\n\n\\[x = 2 + \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = 2 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\]\n\nWe are asked to express these solutions in the form \\(x = \\frac{a \\pm b\\sqrt{c}}{d}\\). Comparing \\(x = 2 + \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\) with \\(\\frac{a \\pm b\\sqrt{c}}{d}\\), we see that \\(a = 6\\), \\(b = 2\\), \\(c = 6\\), and \\(d = 3\\). Therefore, \\(x = \\frac{6 \\pm 2\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\).\n\nThe values of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), \\(c\\), and \\(d\\) are 6, 2, 6, and 3, respectively. Adding these together, we get:\n\n\\[a + b + c + d = 6 + 2 + 6 + 3 = 17.\\]\n\nThus, the value is \\(\\boxed{17}\\).",
